                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Josef Albers, Plate IV-1 from Interaction of Color, 1963,"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                35,
                200,
                424,
                574
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "In visual perception a color is almost never seen as it really is—as it physically is. This fact makes color the most relative medium in art. —JOSEF ALBERS"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                542,
                198,
                892,
                258
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "At the heart of both Interaction of Colorand Albers's color course is the concept of color relativity. On the previous page the two small brown areas are identical in color, but they appear very different from one another because of their relationships with the surrounding colors. Focus your eyes on the line where dark blue and yellow meet, while observing the brown squares in your peripheral vision. Does one appear darker than the other? The same image is shown below with the yellow and dark blue removed to show that the brown is a single color, though it appears to be two different colors when it is surrounded by different background colors. Albers framed the idea of color relativity in an exercise that gives students access to the basic concept quickly and allows them to build upon it following their own curiosity. It can be done in many ways. What follows is a description of one possible approach."
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                539,
                297,
                930,
                535
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "image",
            "content": {
                "image_source": {
                    "path": "images/b2d6a056a98a4cf89fd0c5f2d042a68f54f3d717752b4500cca07fdde7f2df34.jpg"
                },
                "image_caption": [],
                "image_footnote": []
            },
            "bbox": [
                571,
                578,
                806,
                803
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "page_number",
            "content": {
                "page_number_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "4"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                14,
                929,
                22,
                942
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "page_number",
            "content": {
                "page_number_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "5"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                941,
                930,
                949,
                944
            ]
        }
    ],
    [
        {
            "type": "title",
            "content": {
                "title_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Materials"
                    }
                ],
                "level": 2
            },
            "bbox": [
                540,
                81,
                607,
                103
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "image",
            "content": {
                "image_source": {
                    "path": "images/be7f891825b1ec90b060ca99e65d813ae7771d6a49a5a51c0eb6b47f8fc2d48d.jpg"
                },
                "image_caption": [],
                "image_footnote": []
            },
            "bbox": [
                45,
                204,
                430,
                575
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "• Colored paper—the more the better. Albers's students often used a specific brand called Color-aid. Paint swatch samples, wrapping paper, paper from magazines, or construction paper also work well."
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                569,
                203,
                916,
                261
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "• Scissors"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                570,
                266,
                620,
                284
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "• White card stock or sturdy paper"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                569,
                290,
                738,
                308
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "• Glue. Glue sticks are good, as is liquid glue."
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                569,
                314,
                786,
                333
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "• A strip of white paper for each student, approximately two inches wide"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                569,
                338,
                914,
                359
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Having a good selection of colored paper is the best way to start, but don't worry if you can't get your hands on Color-aid or something similar. While Color-aid packets can include as many as 314 colors, having just 2o is enough to begin."
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                539,
                378,
                926,
                439
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Albers was clear that the experiments in Interaction of Color should be done with paper and not paint. Applying paint to paper evenly with clean lines requires a set of skills and takes time, preventing a student from immediately engaging with color. Further, with a wide variety of colored paper in front of them, students can work quickly and change directions easily. Pieces of paper that are around three by four inches work well, but any size can be used. Materials can be shared. A table of eight students could all work from one large set of colored paper."
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                539,
                455,
                930,
                596
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Although the exercise described here uses colored paper, don't let a lack of materials hold you back. Wonderful results cau be discovered by experimenting with fabric, digital tools, and any other colorful material, including paint, despite Albers's direction."
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                539,
                613,
                931,
                691
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Once you've started, you will see color possibilities all around you!"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                539,
                711,
                859,
                730
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "page_number",
            "content": {
                "page_number_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "6"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                22,
                926,
                32,
                941
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "page_number",
            "content": {
                "page_number_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "7"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                942,
                929,
                951,
                942
            ]
        }
    ],
    [
        {
            "type": "title",
            "content": {
                "title_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Getting started"
                    }
                ],
                "level": 2
            },
            "bbox": [
                45,
                78,
                149,
                102
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Spread the papers out so that you can see as many colors as possible at the same time. Choose sets of three colors, arranging them in the orientation used in the demonstration to the right (two vertical pieces in the background with one horizontal across the middle). Place a vertical white strip on top so that the horizontally placed color is visually divided and can be seen interacting separately with the two background colors. Experiment by swapping colors out and observing the differences. In the following example of blue, blue-green, and green, any of the three colors could be changed. The blue could be exchanged for a very pale blue, the green for a yellow, or the blue-green for gray or even red. Experiment and note how the relationship between the colors changes. To preserve supplies, allow this stage of inquiry to continue for a while without using scissors and glue. If you've found something that works, set those colors aside and find another combination that also works, perhaps in a different way."
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                41,
                200,
                436,
                458
            ]
